<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/laptop.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim vocabulary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Property Claim Vocabulary: Terms Defined</h1><p class="lede">43 terms you’ll meet in New York policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="gfilter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the vocabulary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="gcount" aria-live="polite"><span data-gcount>43</span> terms shown</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Jump to letter"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>The value of damaged property after depreciation, usually replacement cost minus wear, age and obsolescence. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expenses (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the increase in living costs, such as temporary housing, while your home is unlivable after a covered loss. It usually sits under Coverage D, loss of use.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It is the question an appraisal panel is generally limited to deciding.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by at least two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss. Most versions descend from the New York Standard Fire Policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-C"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A clause, common in commercial policies, that reduces a payment if the property was insured for less than a set percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Competent and disinterested</dt><dd>The standard an appraiser or umpire must meet under the New York form: qualified to value the loss and without a stake in the outcome.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A (dwelling)</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers the house itself and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B (other structures)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures on the property, such as a garage, shed or fence.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C (personal property)</dt><dd>Coverage for belongings such as furniture, clothing and electronics, often subject to special limits for certain items.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage D (loss of use)</dt><dd>Coverage for additional living expenses or lost rental income while the home cannot be lived in after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, the property, coverage limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition that separates replacement cost from actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Dwelling</dt><dd>The main residence insured under a policy. Its limit often drives percentage deductibles.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-E"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-H"><dt>Hurricane deductible</dt><dd>A deductible, often 1% to 5% of dwelling coverage, that applies only to losses from the hurricane or windstorm event the policy defines. Common on Long Island, in NYC and in coastal Westchester.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-I"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps meltwater, which can then back up under shingles and leak into the home.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Ingress of water</dt><dd>Water entering a building, a phrase often used in policies to distinguish how and where water got in, such as through a roof opening or from the ground.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate describing one task or material, with a quantity and unit price.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Loss assessment coverage</dt><dd>Coverage that helps pay your share of a special assessment a co-op or condo board levies after a covered loss to common property.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-M"><dt>Master policy</dt><dd>The insurance policy a co-op corporation or condo association buys for the building and common areas.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture or style. Treatment varies by policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps taken after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or extracting water. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-N"><dt>NFIP</dt><dd>The National Flood Insurance Program, the federal program that writes most flood policies. Its policy has its own appraisal provision limited to certain disputes about value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>NYPIUA (New York FAIR Plan)</dt><dd>The New York Property Insurance Underwriting Association, the state’s residual market insurer for property owners who cannot find regular coverage.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of repairing to current building codes, such as upgraded electrical or required demolition.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;amp;P)</dt><dd>The general contractor’s charge for managing a job and earning a margin, often expressed as a percentage added to an estimate when several trades are involved.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time after the insurer requests it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A professional licensed by the New York State Department of Financial Services to represent policyholders in presenting and negotiating claims, usually for a percentage fee.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld from an ACV payment that the policy allows you to recover once repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Regulation 64</dt><dd>The New York DFS regulation (11 NYCRR 216) that sets standards for how insurers handle and settle claims.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which an insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what was damaged and what work is needed to repair it, separate from pricing.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Sewer backup endorsement</dt><dd>An optional endorsement that adds coverage for water backing up through sewers or drains, often with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of accumulated snow and ice on a roof. Heavy lake-effect snow in Western and Central New York can stress or collapse roofs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of roofing area equal to 100 square feet.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Subrogation</dt><dd>The insurer’s right, after paying a claim, to recover from a third party who caused the loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy clause limiting how long you have to sue the insurer. Many New York property policies use two years from the loss; check yours.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an estimate after the initial inspection, often once hidden damage is found.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Unit-owners (HO-6) policy</dt><dd>A policy for a condo or co-op owner covering the interior of the unit, improvements, personal property and liability.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors that prices line items using regional cost data.</dd></div></dl></article>
<aside class="side"><div class="side-sticky"><div class="side-box side-call"><p class="side-t">Talk it through</p><p>A first conversation is free. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-orange btn-block" href="/reach-us/">Send claim details</a></div><nav class="side-box side-links" aria-label="Resources"><p class="side-t">Resources</p><ul><li><a href="/appraisal-walkthrough/">Appraisal walkthrough</a></li><li><a href="/ask-an-appraiser/">Ask an appraiser (FAQ)</a></li><li><a href="/claim-vocabulary/" aria-current="page">Claim vocabulary</a></li><li><a href="/policyholder-guides/">Policyholder guides</a></li></ul></nav></div></aside></div>
